WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.690 --> 00:00:01.270
Hello.

00:00:01.270 --> 00:00:03.450
This is the metamorphosis
of the cube.

00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:06.908
I'm professor Erik
Demaine in CSAIL at MIT.

00:00:06.908 --> 00:00:08.700
And this is joint work
with Martin Demaine,

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.510
Ana [INAUDIBLE],, Joseph
O'Rourke, [? Arina ?] Pachinko.

00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:14.610
And we're looking here
at different foldings

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:18.750
and unfoldings of
surfaces in 3D.

00:00:18.750 --> 00:00:21.660
Here we have our friend the
cube and a familiar unfolding

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:24.470
of the cube called
the cross unfolding.

00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:27.660
And it turns out that if you
change the creases in the cross

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:29.820
and fold it up a
different way, you

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:32.310
can make all sorts of
different convex polyhedra.

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:35.460
Here we have a very simple
one, a flat doubly covered

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:36.690
quadrilateral.

00:00:36.690 --> 00:00:39.360
But all the others are
going to be non-flat.

00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:41.700
So we saw the cube,
got the quadrilateral.

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:45.630
The next one is a
pentahedron, five sides.

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:51.930
And we form exactly the surface
of this bilaterally symmetric

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:55.030
pentahedron.

00:00:55.030 --> 00:00:58.720
On the other hand, if we
change the creases in this way,

00:00:58.720 --> 00:01:00.250
then we get a tetrahedron.

00:01:00.250 --> 00:01:02.800
This is my favorite because
the little tab fits exactly

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:06.340
into the pocket at exactly the
surface of that tetrahedron,

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:11.560
not a regular tetrahedron,
but a tetrahedron nonetheless.

00:01:11.560 --> 00:01:15.310
And finally, if we change
the creases in this way,

00:01:15.310 --> 00:01:17.290
we get an octahedron.

00:01:17.290 --> 00:01:20.830
And all of these polyhedra
are computed automatically

00:01:20.830 --> 00:01:23.710
with an algorithm that
given a flat shape

00:01:23.710 --> 00:01:26.380
tells you all of the
different convex polyhedra

00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:28.390
that it can glue up into.

00:01:28.390 --> 00:01:32.020
So here we had five
different shapes, the cube,

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:35.140
the quadrilateral, the
pentahedron, the tetrahedron,

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:36.610
and the octahedron.

00:01:36.610 --> 00:01:40.970
But for different polygons,
you get different 3D shapes.

00:01:40.970 --> 00:01:43.430
Just to give you an idea
of the range of unfolding,

00:01:43.430 --> 00:01:44.683
unfolding is a lot harder.

00:01:44.683 --> 00:01:46.100
If I give you a
3D surface and you

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:49.790
cut it up and try to unfold
it, when does it overlap

00:01:49.790 --> 00:01:51.340
is a big question.

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:54.320
That was an example of a
non-overlapping unfolding.

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:57.960
There's quite a variety
in what you could do.

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:00.690
Here's a particularly fun
unfolding we called the stop

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:02.040
light unfolding of the cube.

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:04.020
It looks like a stop light.

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:08.759
And here's one example of
another 3D convex polyhedron

00:02:08.759 --> 00:02:11.039
you can fold it up into,
which we call the spaceship

00:02:11.039 --> 00:02:13.637
for technical reasons.

00:02:13.637 --> 00:02:15.220
And again, the
algorithm automatically

00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:18.430
computes all the
different 3D convex

00:02:18.430 --> 00:02:22.240
polyhedra you can glue
that flat shape into.

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:25.160
Here we go back to the cube.

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:29.500
And this is ongoing research
in computational geometry.

